msgid "but thought the Tableau limited the strategic potential of the game, so I added an extra deck and experimented with the Tableau layout, aiming for a game that was almost entirely strategic in nature but not on the 10th order of mental magnitude. The result is Sanibel. The number of face-up cards initially dealt to the Tableau determines how much \"luck\" will play a factor in the game. If you only deal 3 or 4 face-up cards to each pile retaining the balance in the Reserve, chances are you will loose some games. Technically, there is nothing wrong with that, and sometimes I will play it this way. On the other hand, dealing every card face up (except the last 4) takes away nothing from the game and only serves to increase the strategy involved. I prefer the 3-down-7-up layout, since the face down cards and the small Reserve give you something immediate to work for, and it can generate a little suspense when you know there is a card buried that you need and you're trying to find a way to uncover it..."
msgstr "但覺得牌列限縮了這遊戲的策略潛力，所以我多加了一副牌，並嘗試各種牌列排列，想要一款幾乎完全講策略、但也不用費神到第十層腦力的遊戲。結果就是聖伊貝爾（Sanibel）。開局發到牌列的正面牌數量，決定「運氣」在遊戲中的影響程度。如果每堆只發 3 或 4 張正面朝上、其餘都留在預備堆，你很可能會輸掉幾局。這沒有不對，我有時也這樣玩。另一方面，除最後 4 張外全部正面朝上發牌，並不會損失遊戲性，只會讓策略成分更高。我偏好 3 張朝下、7 張朝上的排列，因為面朝下的牌與小小的預備堆會給你一個立刻努力的目標，而且當你知道有張你需要的牌埋在下頭、正設法把它翻出來時，還能製造一點懸念..."

